# Usage tracking & cost attribution — {{ .input_project_name }}

Every resource in this project is tagged so usage can be attributed back to the
project, its environment, and each agent in `system.billing.usage`. Tags are for
tracking and monitoring. They don't set a spend limit.

References: [Use tags to attribute and track usage](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/account-settings/usage-detail-tags),
[Serverless usage policies](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/usage/budget-policies).

## Tag scheme

| Tag key | Value | Applied to |
|---|---|---|
| `project` | `{{ .input_project_name }}` | Every taggable resource and the usage policy |
| `environment` | `${bundle.target}` (`dev` / `staging` / `prod`) | Every taggable resource and the usage policy |
| `agent` | agent name | Per-agent MLflow experiments |

Serverless resources (Apps, Vector Search, Lakebase) get `project` and
`environment` only from the serverless usage policy below. Serverless jobs and
pipelines also get billing tags from the policy; their own `tags` label the
resource but aren't relied on for serverless billing. Classic compute (job or
all-purpose clusters) bills under the cluster's `custom_tags`. Experiments carry
the tags themselves. Per-agent app usage is attributed with
`usage_metadata.app_name` in `system.billing.usage` (each agent is its own App).

In `mode: development` (the `dev` target), the bundle also adds a `dev: <your user>`
tag to jobs and pipelines.

