## Serverless usage policy

Serverless compute (Apps, Vector Search, serverless jobs and pipelines, Lakebase) only
picks up custom tags through a **serverless usage policy**. A policy's tags are
stamped onto the serverless usage of every resource that references it. Policies
don't tag classic compute.

If you can use exactly one policy, Databricks applies it to new resources
automatically. If you can use several and none is chosen, the alphabetically
first one is used. Setting `budget_policy_id` explicitly keeps attribution
predictable either way.
{{ if .input_usage_policy_id }}
This project was created with usage policy `{{ .input_usage_policy_id }}`, set as
the `usage_policy_id` variable default in `databricks.yml`.
{{ else }}
No usage policy ID was provided at init, so the `budget_policy_id` lines in
`databricks.yml` and `resources/*.yml` are rendered commented out. Once you have
a policy, wire it in with one command (see [Wire policy IDs into the bundle](#wire-policy-ids-into-the-bundle)).
{{ end }}
### Find an existing policy ID

In the workspace, click your username → **Settings** → **Compute**, and next to
**Serverless usage policies** click **Manage**. Open the policy; its ID is shown
on the policy's page. With an account-level CLI profile you can also run
`databricks account budget-policy list -p <account-profile>`.

The policy must carry the tags `project = {{ .input_project_name }}` and
`environment = <environment>`. Serverless usage (Apps, Vector Search, Lakebase)
takes its tags from the policy only, so an untagged policy attaches without
errors and attributes nothing. To check, open the policy (see above), or look
at an app's **Settings** page: it shows "Selected policy has no tags" under the
policy if it has none.

### Create a policy

Creating a policy requires the **workspace admin** role, or the **Serverless usage
policy: Manager** permission. Viewing and managing every policy in the account
additionally requires the account-level **Billing admin** role.

**Option A: workspace settings** (or send these steps to a workspace admin):

1. Click your username in the top bar → **Settings** → **Compute**.
2. Next to **Serverless usage policies**, click **Manage**, then **Create**.
3. Name it `{{ .input_project_name }}-<environment>` and add the custom tags
   `project = {{ .input_project_name }}` and `environment = <environment>`.
4. Repeat for each environment you want to split usage by.
5. Wire the IDs in (next section).

**Option B: script** (needs an account-level CLI profile, since the CLI only
exposes the account API):

```bash
databricks auth login --host <accounts-console-url> --account-id <account-id> -p my-account
./scripts/create_usage_policy.sh my-account dev staging prod
```

This creates `{{ .input_project_name }}-dev`, `-staging`, and `-prod` policies tagged with
`project` and `environment`, then wires their IDs into the bundle (next section).

### Wire policy IDs into the bundle

```bash
# One policy per environment (recommended):
python3 scripts/enable_usage_policy.py dev=<dev-id> staging=<staging-id> prod=<prod-id>
# Or one shared policy for every target:
python3 scripts/enable_usage_policy.py --default <policy-id>
```

The script sets `usage_policy_id` under each target in `databricks.yml` and
uncomments every `# budget_policy_id: ${var.usage_policy_id}` line in
`databricks.yml` and `resources/*.yml`. If any target would be left without an
ID, it exits without changing anything, so no target deploys with an empty
policy. `create_usage_policy.sh` runs this step for you. You can re-run it to
change IDs.

### Grant access

Users and service principals can only attach policies they have the **User**
role on. Open each policy (workspace **Settings → Compute → Serverless usage
policies → Manage**), go to the **Permissions** tab, click **Grant access**, and grant
**User** to:

- the developers who deploy to `dev`
- the service principals that deploy to `staging` and `prod` (see `docs/setup.md`)

Without this grant, `databricks bundle deploy` fails with a permission error on
`budget_policy_id`.

Granting access doesn't attach the policy to resources that already exist.
Deploy the bundle with `budget_policy_id` set to attach it.

### Per-environment policies

A policy's tags are fixed, so you need one policy per environment for serverless
usage to carry the right `environment` tag. Set the ID under each target in
`databricks.yml`:

```yaml
targets:
  prod:
    variables:
      usage_policy_id: <prod-usage-policy-id>
```

### Serverless pipelines

Pipelines in this project are serverless (`serverless: true`). For any serverless
pipeline, keep in mind:

- A pipeline triggered by a job doesn't inherit the job's policy. Set
  `budget_policy_id` on the pipeline itself.
- In development mode (the `dev` target), policy tag
  changes take up to 24 hours to reach new pipeline updates.

### Deleted policies

A resource keeps a deleted policy's ID, and deploys still succeed, but its usage
is no longer tagged. If tags disappear, check that the policy still exists.

## Adding new resources

Keep new resources consistent with the scheme:

| New resource | Add |
|---|---|
| Job cluster (`job_clusters[].new_cluster`) | `custom_tags: {project: ..., environment: ${bundle.target}}` |
| Existing all-purpose cluster (`existing_cluster_id`) | Set the same `custom_tags` on the cluster itself; the job can't tag it |
| Serverless job (no cluster set) | `budget_policy_id: ${var.usage_policy_id}` for billing, plus job `tags` to label the job |
| Serverless pipeline (`serverless: true`) | Pipeline `tags` plus `budget_policy_id: ${var.usage_policy_id}` (job-triggered pipelines don't inherit the job's policy) |
| SQL warehouse | `tags: {custom_tags: [{key: project, value: ...}, {key: environment, value: ${bundle.target}}]}` (usage policies don't cover warehouses) |
| Model serving endpoint | `tags: [{key: project, value: ...}, ...]` plus `budget_policy_id: ${var.usage_policy_id}` |
| New agent | Run `add_agent.py` / `add_supervisor.py`. They add the experiment tags and app policy automatically. |

Limits: at most 20 custom tags per resource. Keys and values may only contain
letters, digits, and `+ - = . , _ : @` (no spaces or `/`).

## Query usage

Tags appear in `system.billing.usage` within a few hours of usage (tags added
later are not applied retroactively).

```sql
SELECT
  u.usage_date,
  u.custom_tags['environment']      AS environment,
  u.billing_origin_product           AS product,
  u.usage_metadata.app_name          AS app_name,
  SUM(u.usage_quantity)              AS dbus,
  SUM(u.usage_quantity * p.pricing.effective_list.default) AS est_list_cost
FROM system.billing.usage u
LEFT JOIN system.billing.list_prices p
  ON u.sku_name = p.sku_name
 AND u.cloud = p.cloud
 AND u.usage_start_time >= p.price_start_time
 AND (p.price_end_time IS NULL OR u.usage_end_time <= p.price_end_time)
WHERE u.custom_tags['project'] = '{{ .input_project_name }}'
  AND u.usage_date >= current_date() - INTERVAL 30 DAYS
GROUP BY ALL
ORDER BY u.usage_date DESC, est_list_cost DESC;
```
